<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1242–<a href="/years/1277">1277</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as John of Lüneburg · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">House of Welf</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John, Duke of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a>, was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-the-child">Otto the Child</a>. When he and his brother Albert the Tall divided the duchy in 1269, John received Lüneburg and became the first prince of the Principality of Lüneburg. His line, the Old House of Lüneburg, ruled there until 1369.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>John belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">House of Welf</a>. His father, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-the-child">Otto the Child</a>, had received the Welf allodial possessions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> from Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a> and become the first Duke of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a>. After Otto's death in 1252 John ruled the duchy jointly with his elder brother, Albert the Tall. In 1265 he married Liutgard, a daughter of Count Gerhard I of Holstein-Itzehoe, with whom he had five children.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Because the brothers could not agree who should govern, they decided in 1267 to divide their possessions, and in 1269 John received the right to choose his part. He chose the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a> estates with the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, while Albert received Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfenbuttel">Wolfenbüttel</a> with further lands around Calenberg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottingen">Göttingen</a>. John thus founded the Old Line of Lüneburg, although under imperial law the duchy remained undivided and all the Welf princes kept the title Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg.</p><p>In a fierce feud with Count Gunzelin III of Schwerin, John acquired the settlement of Uelzen, whose residents he granted town privileges in 1270. He tried in vain to gain control of the Lüneburg Saltworks and in the end gave the citizens of Lüneburg a monopoly of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salt-trade">salt trade</a> in his principality, carried mainly along the Old Salt Route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>John died on 13 December 1277 and was buried in the cloister of St. Michael's Church at his residence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a>. His son and heir, Otto II the Strict, was still a minor and remained under the tutelage of his uncles, Duke Albert the Tall and Bishop Conrad of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verden">Verden</a>, until 1282. Of his daughters, Matilde married Henry I, Prince of Mecklenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustrow">Güstrow</a>, and Elisabeth married John II, Count of Oldenburg; an illegitimate son, Henry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a>, became a canon in Walsrode.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Brunswick, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.27,10.52&amp;year=1264" aria-label="Brunswick on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4787 359 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of John, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5237 629h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5237 629h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=52.27,10.52&amp;year=1264">What was here in 1264? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
